WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.380
Game of Thrones fans can and will find plenty
of reasons to feel outraged over an episode's

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:06.640
embrace of darkness.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.520
Viewers kicked, screamed, cried, and begged
for comfort after watching the "Rains of Castamere"

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.040
episode from season 3, better known as the
installment featuring the Red Wedding.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.440
Audiences once again felt their stomachs churn
when Prince Oberyn Martell got his brains

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.460
bashed in during season 4's "The Mountain
and the Viper," and felt a similar feeling

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.350
of dread when Shireen Baratheon was burned
alive on the season 5 episode "The Dance of

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:28.220
Dragons."

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:32.020
While watching the third episode of season
8, titled "The Long Night," fans took to social

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.519
media to air their latest grievances about
the show.

00:00:34.519 --> 00:00:38.339
Now, while the practice of criticizing on
Twitter is nothing new for the Game of Thrones

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:42.880
fandom, the April 28th episode elicited a
rather unique, highly specific complaint.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.120
According to fans, the episode was just too
dark, in the most literal way.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.620
Not long after “The Long Night” began,
Twitter exploded with complaints about how

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.120
dark the much-anticipated battle sequence
was.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.900
For many, the episode was nearly unwatchable
because the lighting was so dim.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.460
Variety writer Caroline Darya Framke tweeted
mid-episode:

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.640
"Sure we can't see anything on #GameofThrones
without squinting, but the thing is, it's

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.300
DARK at night and the ice dragon show is extremely
committed to realism."

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.520
News reporter Courtney Theriault had the perfect
response to the frustrating darkness, tweeting:

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:16.291
"After watching #BattleOfWinterfell, I honestly
don't know if I'll ever be able to look at

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.260
a sunny sky again without going blind."

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.580
One user posted an impassioned tweet asking
television executives to think of the audience

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.910
before deciding how to light each episode,
writing:

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:28.480
"Dear people making TV shows, THE VIEWERS
AT HOME WOULD PREFER IF THEY COULD SEE WHAT

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.320
WAS HAPPENING ON SCREEN STOP MAKING EVERYTHING
SO DARK.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.040
Sincerely, Everyone watching tonight's #GameOfThrones."

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.480
Another Twitter user also expressed increasing
frustration over not being able to fully see

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:41.420
everything that was going down during the
Battle of Winterfell, tweeting:

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.260
"No clue what's happening in Game of Thrones
cause it's too dark.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:47.650
I have adjusted the settings on my TV and
it still didn't help.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:50.490
Guess I will be reading show summaries tomorrow
bright and early.”

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.860
Another fan took direct aim at HBO, slamming
the network for spending enormous amounts

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:58.681
of money on episodes where viewers have little
idea what’s unfolding due to how dark it

00:01:58.681 --> 00:01:59.300
is:

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:03.800
"HBO: Let's make a show about dragons and
zombies and spend a gazillion dollars on it.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:07.580
Also HBO: Let's make it so dark literally
no one can tell what's happening."

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.960
Fortunately, not every Game of Thrones fan
took issue with the lighting choice.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.940
Many viewers started firing back on Twitter
at those bashing the episode, arguing that

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.040
no one should have been surprised by the lack
of light.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.960
After all, the episode was literally titled
"The Long Night."

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:23.409
And as we know, nighttime has historically
been pretty dark:

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:26.060
"Game of Thrones has been visually dark since
day 1.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.299
It's funny that people are upset now like
it's new.

00:02:28.299 --> 00:02:32.140
I figured last night was shot the way that
it was to add to the chaos for the viewer.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.849
The characters didn't know [what] was going
on and neither did we.”

00:02:34.849 --> 00:02:35.959
Another user wrote:

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:37.889
"Stop barking that it was too dark!

00:02:37.889 --> 00:02:41.720
You better buy some high quality TV or should
have watch it on HD you fools!

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.139
It was meant to be dark cinematography because
it happened during dark winter.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:48.660
The characters also had a hard time seeing
so it was actually immersive."

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.600
ScreenRant features editor Hannah Shaw-Williams
had perhaps the most level-headed reaction

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:53.639
to the criticism.

00:02:53.639 --> 00:02:57.439
According to her, those who found fault with
"The Long Night" for being incredibly dark

00:02:57.439 --> 00:03:00.819
are entitled to their opinion, and those who
had no trouble making out everything that

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:04.040
happened shouldn't "shout down" those who
didn’t have the same experience.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.240
The editor tweeted:

00:03:05.249 --> 00:03:08.720
"... Like, do I need to get out the histograms
to prove how insanely under-lit this show

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:09.380
is?

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:12.159
I feel like this is some kind of weird mass
gas-lighting campaign.

00:03:12.159 --> 00:03:17.019
Btw if you are gas-lighting, please send your
gas lights to HBO bc they clearly need them."

00:03:17.019 --> 00:03:21.230
Overall, "The Long Night" was a pretty epic
episode despite some major events being difficult

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:21.800
to see.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.739
Melisandre returned to Winterfell and ignited
the Dothraki's weapons with fire courtesy

00:03:25.739 --> 00:03:30.180
of the Lord of Light, the dead rose in the
crypts of Winterfell, Theon Greyjoy heroically

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.299
sacrificed his life to try and defend Bran
Stark, a few major characters died during

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:38.109
battle, and Arya Stark went full Prince That
Was Promised by killing the Night King with

00:03:38.109 --> 00:03:40.240
her dagger made of Valyrian steel.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.840
After all… she knows the true secrets of
combat:

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:45.400
"Stick them with the pointy end."

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.280
At the end of the day, when you play the game
of thrones, you either win or you die.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:53.800
And when you watch Game of Thrones, you either
invest in some night-vision goggles to see

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.489
what's happening or you strain your eyes trying.

